# Eponym common library: single-use handles / affine-linear types (issue #482).
#
# A one-shot acked job. The protocol, in order, exactly once each:
# fresh --sendJ--> sent --ackJ--> acked --closeJ--> closed. A handle
# is a q1 (linear) value: inside checked code, an ANNOTATED binder
# for it cannot be used twice and cannot be dropped silently
# (Issue #66). The same holds through LET-bound intermediates (Issue
# #484, resolved): a let is checked under both counts — the value
# evaluates once, so its uses count unconditionally, and the value's
# context is also scaled by the bound variable's use count in the
# body — so `let s = sendJ zero j in zero` (drop) and
# `let s = sendJ zero j in (ackJ s, ackJ s)` (double use) are both
# compile-time errors (examples/ep/29d, 29e), as is passing a
# let-bound linear-derived value to a q0 parameter. No annotated-let
# form or new syntax was needed: the answer to #484's open question is
# the conjunction of the strict evaluation count with the QTT let
# rule, and lets over pure values are unaffected.
# So the single-use guarantee covers threaded clients in their normal
# let-bound style, not only directly threaded annotated binders.
# (see the watchlist in examples/embedded/linear_handles.nim).
# The phases are what Nim
# alone cannot enforce: a destructor gives exactly-once destruction,
# but it cannot enforce send-before-ack, ack-before-close, or
# exactly-once send. That ordering-plus-completion obligation is what
# the q1 checking earns its keep on; a handle with only a
# "close exactly once" obligation would be vacuous, since Nim alone
# suffices for that.
#
# Honest boundary (see docs/known-limitations.md, "Static checks do not
# manage Nim resources"): quantities apply inside checked terms. They
# do not inspect aliases or ownership in surrounding Nim code. ORC's
# deterministic drop is trusted runtime glue, not something the checker
# verifies. This library demonstrates checking at the eponym API
# boundary; it does not claim end-to-end resource management.
#
# Typed boundary: Nim code is an omega context. Only omega values cross
# to Nim; a live q1 handle crosses only through an explicit conversion.
# For this handle there is deliberately NO copy-to-omega method, and
# each variant fails for a stated reason:
# - Consuming copy (move), (q1 x : R) -> omega S: the "copy" would be a
#   second handle onto the same backend job, so Nim could re-send or
#   close through the alias. Transferring ownership does not help: the
#   omega value is freely reusable by design, so the protocol is lost.
# - Non-consuming deep copy: sound only for genuinely independent
#   values (no shared mutable state). A job handle fronts shared
#   backend state (the queue entry), so an independent copy does not
#   exist; copying the handle value would alias that state.
# - Relabeling: the same pointer with an omega tag is unsound, full
#   stop; it is the caveat with a nicer name.
# The sound boundary for a live protocol handle is therefore the
# bracket, not a copy: withJob takes an eponym-checked callback, runs it
# with a fresh handle, and only the callback's omega result (a Nat
# receipt) crosses back to Nim. Copy-to-omega is the simplest case of
# that encapsulation, and it is what the completed receipt is: an
# immutable value, not a handle. Copy-to-omega does NOT buy Nim-side
# single use: if a use case needs Nim to use something exactly once (a
# non-replayable capability), do not expose the value at all; expose
# operations that run inside eponym, as withJob does.
#
# Bracket shape, stated exactly: withJob fixes the callback's result
# type to Nat, as withBorrow does (lib/borrow.ep). With the result type
# fixed to a value type, there is no channel by which the handle could
# outlive the bracket. The generic-result variant
#   Pi(A: Univ, Pi(k: Pi(1 j: Job freshP, A), A))
# was considered and NOT shipped: the caller instantiates A, so nothing
# stops A := Job freshP and the callback returning the handle, which
# escapes. That is the runST gap: runST's soundness comes from the
# state parameter being quantified INSIDE the bracket's argument type
# (forall s. ST s a), so the callback is polymorphic in s and its
# result type a, fixed outside, cannot mention s. This fragment has no
# such higher-rank quantification over the handle, and protocol
# operations need concrete phase indices, so a rank-2 callback could
# not call them anyway. The Nat-fixed bracket is the sound shape
# available here; the generic bracket is a watchlist item, not a
# shipped method.
#
# Representation verdict (see examples/embedded/linear_handles.nim for
# the measurement): NEGATIVE for this handle shape. Phase indices erase
# at runtime (every Job phase maps to the same Nim ref type) and
# quantities never reach codegen, so the q1 handle and the omega handle
# are the same runtime value moved the same way. Linearity here is
# checking-only. A linearity-exploiting representation (a q1 handle
# compiled to a non-refcounted owned pointer, or a q1 argument compiled
# to a sink move) would require codegen to map q1 to ownership, which
# it does not do today; that is #413's move-semantics territory.
import "nat.ep"

# Phases of a one-shot job's lifecycle.
data(JobPhase, Univ) where
| freshP : JobPhase
| sentP : JobPhase
| ackedP : JobPhase
| closedP : JobPhase

# A job handle indexed by its current phase.
data(Job, JobPhase -> Univ) where
| mkJob (p : JobPhase) : Job p

# Lifecycle operations. Each consumes the handle in its required phase
# and delivers it in the next phase; the index change is the transition.
# sendJ takes the payload first, as sendH does in lib/typestate.ep.
postulate(sendJ, Pi(x: Nat, Pi(1 j: Job freshP, Job sentP)))
postulate(ackJ, Pi(1 j: Job sentP, Sig(a: Nat, Job ackedP)))
postulate(closeJ, Pi(1 j: Job ackedP, Job closedP))

# Bracket: the callback is an eponym-checked term taking a fresh linear
# handle and computing a Nat receipt; the handle never becomes a value
# Nim code can name. The callback must be written in eponym: a plain
# Nim proc callback would receive the handle as an ordinary Nim value
# and could stash it, double-use it, or drop it unchecked. A Nim func
# callback cannot do the job either: it cannot perform the side-effect
# operations at all, and confinement is not linearity (it does not
# enforce exactly-once use). The backend creates the handle, runs the
# callback, and requires the protocol to have completed (closed) when
# the callback returns; anything else is a backend error.
postulate(withJob, Pi(k: Pi(1 j: Job freshP, Nat), Nat))
